					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/theflamingle" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6319959); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=6319959&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605795173" alt="TheFlamingle" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=6319959)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/theflamingle" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6319959); return false;" >TheFlamingle</a></span> <span class="odate time_1588020964 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">27 Apr 2020 20:56</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4617679">
									

<p>This article has its strengths. Well conceived, no shortage of effort, properly formatted, and the phenomenon is relatively interesting if straight forward.</p>
<p>What gums it up for me is the lacking tone. Many parts of this don't read like a Foundation file: &quot;Though the responses were vague, people answered that it was a festival for all of them.&quot; This is just one of many examples that don't read right. Something like &quot;When questioned, individuals only offered that it was 'a festival for all of us'&quot; would be more appropriate.</p>
<p>The journal entries of Dr. Pasqua suffer from a similar issue, in that they just don't read believably to me. The 'interview' with SCP-5770-1 is where it completely drops off the cliff in terms of realism/tone.</p>
<p>I want to like this more, but it needs a rewrite. -1 for now.</p>

							</div>

								
					
